"Timbre and Harmonics | VirtualDemos",
"Timbre and Harmonics",
"<strong>Setups in the demo</strong> (the Presets chips)<strong>:</strong> Square wave; Sawtooth wave; Triangle wave; Like a clarinet; Like a flute; Missing fundamental; Wave packet. The demo opens on Sawtooth wave with f₁ = {#} Hz (A3); Square wave, Triangle wave and Missing fundamental also set {#} Hz, Like a clarinet sets D3 ({#} Hz) and Like a flute A4 ({#} Hz).",
"The picture is a harmonic synthesizer: {#} bars for the amplitudes of harmonics {#} to {#} with a phase dial under each, the spectrum (its dots can be dragged to set levels in dB), the waveform (the sum, with its repetition period bracketed; drag the end of the bracket to change f₁) and a ladder showing each harmonic as a mode of a <strong>String</strong>, <strong>Open pipe</strong> or <strong>Closed pipe</strong>. Drag the bars (sweep across several to paint them) and the dials. Keys: left and right arrows select a harmonic, up and down change its amplitude by {#} ({#} with Shift), the bracket keys turn its phase by {#}°, {#} or Delete silences it.",
"<strong>Fundamental f₁</strong> moves in steps of one cent from three octaves below A4 to one octave above it, and the piano keyboard plays C3 to C5 in equal temperament. Buttons: <strong>Pure tone</strong> (harmonic {#} alone), <strong>All equal</strong>, <strong>Randomize phases</strong>, <strong>All phases zero</strong>; ticks <strong>Remove harmonic {#} (the fundamental)</strong> and <strong>Also remove harmonic {#}</strong>. The <strong>Compare instruments</strong> slider blends five illustrative spectra (flute, clarinet, oboe, trumpet and bowed string like).",
"Readouts: <strong>Period T₁ = {#}/f₁</strong> and where harmonic {#} lies; <strong>Waveform repeats every</strong> and <strong>Pitch from the period</strong>; the <strong>Strongest partials</strong> table, the six strongest harmonics with their frequencies and levels relative to the strongest, which is {#} dB; <strong>Peak / RMS</strong> and <strong>RMS (bar units)</strong>. The folded Inharmonic partials section has <strong>Detune harmonic</strong> ({#} to {#}) <strong>by Δf</strong> (−{#} to {#} Hz in {#} Hz steps), <strong>Stiffness B</strong> ({#} to {#}), the readouts <strong>Shape cycles every</strong> and <strong>Partial {#} / {#} f₁</strong>, and <strong>Back to exact harmonics</strong>.",
"<strong>Record harmonics {#} to {#}</strong> adds six rows for the tone as it is now: the run number and preset name, n, frequency, amplitude relative to the strongest partial, level in dB ({#} dB for the strongest, “off” for a silent harmonic) and phase in degrees, with the RMS, peak/RMS, repetition period, f₁, B, Δf and any removed harmonics in the notes of the first row. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> fills the Data table in order: Run or preset, n, Frequency, Relative amplitude, Level, Phase, notes.",
"With <strong>Sound</strong> on you hear exactly the drawn sum, {#} sine oscillators, scaled to one RMS level so the presets compare fairly; harmonics above {#} kHz play {#} dB quieter.",
